    <h1>House MD: The Greatest Sherlock Holmes Adaptation Ever That's Barely an Adaptation?</h1>

    <h2>
        Well first of all, we have to look at similar adaptations of Sherlock Holmes, or more specifically, its counterparts in the 2000s.
    </h2>

    <ul>
        <li>Elementary</li>
        <li>Sherlock Holmes</li>
        <li>Sherlock Holmes: A Game of Shadows</li>
        <li>Sherlock bbc</li>
        <li>Enola Holmes</li>
        <li>mr holmes</li>
        <li>watson</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
What do all these stories have in common? They all keep Sherlock Holmes mostly the same with only small changes,
or they are treated as direct adaptations of the books, and that's great.
    </p>

    <p>
        But we aren't discussing the quality of the original books. No, we're discussing adaptations,
        and unfortunately, as adaptations, the previously listed works are all too similar to the original sources.
    </p>

    <p>
        House M.D. is different. It doesn't just adapt the books, it changes everything. Rather than being a detective,
        Sherlock Holmes becomes a doctor named Gregory House. His closest friend isn't John Watson, it's James Wilson.
        A fundamental aspect of the adaptation is how different it is from other Holmes adaptations.
    </p>

    <p>
        That is why it's so good. It isn't a straight one-to-one adaptation. It changes almost everything about the
        story, yet still feels unmistakably like a Sherlock Holmes story.
    </p>

